  "full_response": "Python and Go (Golang) are both popular choices for building web APIs, but they have distinct differences in terms of performance, syntax, concurrency, ecosystem, and use cases. Here’s a detailed comparison:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Performance**\n- **Go**:\n  - **Compiled language**: Go compiles directly to machine code, making it significantly faster than interpreted languages like Python.\n  - **Low latency**: Ideal for high-performance APIs (e.g., microservices, real-time systems).\n  - **Efficient memory management**: Uses a garbage collector but is optimized for low overhead.\n\n- **Python**:\n  - **Interpreted language**: Slower than Go due to runtime interpretation (though PyPy or Cython can help).\n  - **Higher latency**: Not ideal for ultra-low-latency APIs unless optimized with extensions (e.g., C/C++ bindings).\n  - **Memory usage**: Generally higher than Go due to dynamic typing and interpreter overhead.\n\n**Winner**: Go for performance-critical APIs.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Concurrency**\n- **Go**:\n  - **Goroutines**: Lightweight threads managed by the Go runtime (cheap to create, thousands can run concurrently).\n  - **Channels**: Built-in support for communication between goroutines (CSP-style concurrency).\n  - **Scalability**: Excellent for handling thousands of concurrent connections (e.g., WebSockets, high-traffic APIs).\n\n- **Python**:\n  - **Threading limitations**: Due to the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL), threads are not truly parallel for CPU-bound tasks.\n  - **Asyncio**: Supports async/await for I/O-bound tasks (e.g., `aiohttp`, FastAPI), but not as efficient as Go’s goroutines.\n  - **Multiprocessing**: Can bypass the GIL but has higher overhead.\n\n**Winner**: Go for high-concurrency APIs.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Syntax and Development Speed**\n- **Go**:\n  - **Minimalist syntax**: Fewer features (no classes, generics until Go 1.18, no exceptions), which can reduce complexity but may feel restrictive.\n  - **Explicit error handling**: Forces developers to handle errors explicitly (no exceptions).\n  - **Faster compilation**: Quick feedback loop during development.\n\n- **Python**:\n  - **Expressive syntax**: More concise and flexible (e.g., list comprehensions, dynamic typing, duck typing).\n  - **Rapid prototyping**: Easier",
